# Onboarding progress

**This file is what makes setup survivable.** If the session dies mid-interview — the laptop
sleeps, they get pulled into a meeting, the context fills — the next session reads this and picks
up exactly where this one stopped.

🔴 **A wave is `done` only when BOTH are true: they confirmed your read-back of it, AND you wrote
their answers below.** Marking a wave `done` without recording the answers is the one thing that
breaks this file, because the next session is told not to re-ask completed waves — so it will
invent what it cannot find, and nothing downstream will catch it.

This is why the file is exempt from "write nothing before the confirmation gate": a wave is only
recorded *after* they confirmed it, so nothing unconfirmed lands here. It stays on their machine.

| Wave | What it covers | Status | Date |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | What to call me, persona, tone | not started | |
| 2 | Who they are, role, timezone, mission | not started | |
| 3 | What to take off their plate | not started | |
| 4 | What I must never do, guardrails | not started | |
| 5 | Their people | not started | |
| 6 | The workflow worth automating | not started | |
| 7 | How they work, and how they want output | not started | |
| 8 | Read it all back | not started | |
| seed | Wiki seed from connectors (`SEED-WIKI.md`) | not started | |

**Waves 1-4 are the minimum viable agent.** Setup stops there. Waves 5-8 are homework day 1.

🔴 **The `seed` row is not a wave — it is the connector crawl, and it is the one row that can be
expensive to redo.** If it reads `in progress`, a previous session was interrupted mid-crawl:
read `.seed-staging/_progress.md` and resume from there rather than re-pulling everything. A value
of `declined` means the seed was offered and refused; leave it, and do not re-offer during setup.

